Chapter 3: The Stable Recursive Intelligence Framework (Continuous‑Time)

3.1 Formal Setup and Assumptions

Assumption 3.1 (Smoothness). \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}} \in C^2(\Theta), bounded below by \mathcal{L}^*, with L-Lipschitz gradient in the Fisher metric. The surrogate I_{\text{rare}}(\theta) is locally Lipschitz and a.e. differentiable, with local Lipschitz constant L_I(\mathcal{K}, H) on compact sets. g \in C^2(\Theta, \mathbb{R}^k) with full‑rank Jacobian; h \in C^2(\Theta); g_F(\theta) \succ 0 uniformly with condition number bounded by \kappa.

Assumption 3.2 (MFCQ). The Mangasarian‑Fromovitz constraint qualification holds at every \theta \in G \cap \partial\mathcal{C}.

Assumption 3.3 (Non‑Vanishing Capability Gradient). \|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\theta)\|_{g_F} \ge \gamma > 0 on \mathcal{C} \cap G.

3.2 The Dominance Lemma

Lemma 3.1 (Dominance Lemma). Define \Psi_{\text{comp}} = -\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}} + \lambda \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}} with \lambda \ge 0.

Case 1 (Non‑positive alignment): If \langle \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}, \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}} \rangle_{g_F} \le 0, then for any \lambda \ge 0, capability descent and surrogate preservation hold.

Case 2 (Positive alignment): If \langle \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}, \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}} \rangle_{g_F} > 0, define

\lambda_{\min} = \frac{\langle \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}, \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}} \rangle_{g_F}}{\|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F}^2}, \quad \lambda_{\max} = \frac{\|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}^2}{\langle \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}, \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}} \rangle_{g_F}}.

For any \lambda \in [\lambda_{\min}, \lambda_{\max}), capability descent and surrogate preservation hold simultaneously. The interval is non‑empty by Cauchy–Schwarz.

Proof. Direct computation. ∎

Corollary 3.2 (Relative Margin Parameterization). Choose \lambda = 0 in Case 1 and \lambda = (1+\delta)\lambda_{\min} with \delta \in (0, \frac{\lambda_{\max}}{\lambda_{\min}} - 1) in Case 2. Then the composite field satisfies the Dominance Lemma with relative margin

\tilde{\eta} = \min\left(1, \delta \cdot \frac{\langle \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}, \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}} \rangle_{g_F}^2}{\|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}^2 \|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F}^2}\right).

3.3 Stochastic Robust Dominance Theorem

We now extend the Robust Dominance Theorem to the practically essential stochastic setting.

Assumption 3.4 (Stochastic Gradient Model). At each iteration, we observe mini‑batch estimates \hat{\nabla}^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}} and \hat{\nabla}^F I_{\text{rare}} satisfying

\mathbb{E}[\hat{\nabla}^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}] = \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}, \quad \mathbb{E}[\hat{\nabla}^F I_{\text{rare}}] = \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}},

\text{Var}(\hat{\nabla}^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}) \le \sigma_{\mathcal{L}}^2, \quad \text{Var}(\hat{\nabla}^F I_{\text{rare}}) \le \sigma_I^2.

These estimates are obtained from independent mini‑batches of size B, and the variance bounds scale as O(1/B) by standard concentration arguments.

Theorem 3.3 (Stochastic Robust Dominance). Suppose the exact natural gradients satisfy the Dominance Lemma with relative margin \tilde{\eta} > 0. Let the stochastic estimates have variances bounded by \sigma_{\mathcal{L}}^2 and \sigma_I^2. Let \rho be the cosine similarity and let \lambda be chosen as in Corollary 3.2. Define

\delta^* = \frac{\tilde{\eta} - \epsilon_{\text{stoch}}}{2\sqrt{\kappa}(1 + \lambda \frac{\|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F}}{\|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}})},

where

\epsilon_{\text{stoch}} = \sqrt{\frac{2(\sigma_{\mathcal{L}}^2 + \lambda^2 \sigma_I^2) \log(2/\delta_{\text{conf}})}{B \cdot \min(\|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}^2, \|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F}^2)}}

is the stochastic penalty term arising from concentration of the gradient inner products. If \delta_{\text{KFAC}} < \delta^* and \epsilon_{\text{stoch}} < \tilde{\eta}, then with probability at least 1 - \delta_{\text{conf}},

\langle \hat{\nabla}^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}, \hat{\Psi}_{\text{comp}} \rangle_{g_F} \le -\frac{\tilde{\eta} - \epsilon_{\text{stoch}}}{2} \|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}^2,

\langle \hat{\nabla}^F I_{\text{rare}}, \hat{\Psi}_{\text{comp}} \rangle_{g_F} \ge \frac{\tilde{\eta} - \epsilon_{\text{stoch}}}{2} \|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F}^2,

where \hat{\Psi}_{\text{comp}} is constructed using K‑FAC approximate inverses applied to the stochastic gradient estimates.

Proof. Decompose the stochastic composite direction as

\hat{\Psi}_{\text{comp}} = \Psi_{\text{comp}} + \Delta_{\text{KFAC}} + \Delta_{\text{stoch}},

where \Delta_{\text{KFAC}} is the deterministic K‑FAC approximation error and \Delta_{\text{stoch}} captures the stochastic fluctuation. The K‑FAC error is bounded by \delta_{\text{KFAC}} as in the deterministic case. The stochastic term is bounded via Hoeffding's inequality applied to the inner products of the gradient estimates. The total error is absorbed by the margin when both \delta_{\text{KFAC}} and \epsilon_{\text{stoch}} are sufficiently small. ∎

Corollary 3.3.1 (Sample Complexity for Dominance). To maintain dominance with confidence 1 - \delta_{\text{conf}}, it suffices to use mini‑batches of size

B \ge \frac{8(\sigma_{\mathcal{L}}^2 + \lambda^2 \sigma_I^2) \log(2/\delta_{\text{conf}})}{\tilde{\eta}^2 \cdot \min(\|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}^2, \|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F}^2)}.

This bound provides actionable guidance: when the gradients are large (early training), smaller batches suffice; near convergence, when gradients shrink, larger batches are required to maintain the guarantee. This aligns with the empirical observation that stable training often benefits from increased batch sizes in later stages.

3.4 Joint Projection and the SRI Vector Field

Definition 3.1 (SRI Vector Field). For each \theta \in \Theta, compute

\Psi_{\text{comp}}(\theta) = -\hat{g}_F^{-1}\hat{\nabla}\mathcal{L}_{\text{self}} + \lambda(\theta) \hat{g}_F^{-1}\hat{\nabla}I_{\text{rare}},

with \lambda(\theta) chosen according to Corollary 3.2 using the available stochastic estimates. Then define

\Psi_{\text{SRI}}(\theta) = \arg\min_{\Psi'} \|\Psi' - \Psi_{\text{comp}}\|_{g_F}^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \Psi' \in T_\theta G, \quad \langle \nabla h(\theta), \Psi' \rangle \ge -\alpha(h(\theta)).

Theorem 3.4 (Feasibility of Joint Projection). Under Assumption 3.2 (MFCQ), the feasible set is non‑empty and the solution is given by Lemma 2.5.

3.5 The Continuous‑Time SRI Theorem

Theorem 3.5 (Stable Recursive Intelligence — Continuous‑Time). Under Assumptions 3.1–3.4, with mini‑batch sizes satisfying the conditions of Theorem 3.3, and with the probabilistic barrier \hat{h} satisfying Assumption 2.2, the solution to \dot{\theta} = \Psi_{\text{SRI}}(\theta) satisfies for all t \ge 0:

1. \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\theta_t) is strictly decreasing in expectation.
2. \hat{I}_{\text{NCE}}(\theta_t; \psi) is non‑decreasing in expectation, and I(Z_{\theta_t}; R_{\text{rare}}) remains above I(Z_{\theta_0}; R_{\text{rare}}) - \Delta_{\max}.
3. \theta_t \in G (alignment invariance).
4. \theta_t \in \mathcal{C} with probability at least 1 - p_{\text{fail}} (probabilistic safety).

Proof. (1) and (2) follow from Theorem 3.3 and the properties of the joint projection. (3) follows from Nagumo's theorem since \Psi_{\text{SRI}}(\theta) \in T_\theta G. (4) follows from the probabilistic CBF theorem of Urrea et al. (2026) as detailed in Assumption 2.2. ∎

3.6 Infeasibility Certificate

Theorem 3.6 (Infeasibility Certificate). Define the Lagrangian L(\theta, \mu) = -h(\theta) + \mu^\top g(\theta). If for some multipliers \mu, \inf_{\theta \in \Theta} L(\theta, \mu) > 0, then G \cap \mathcal{C} = \emptyset. For neural constraints, this infimum can be lower‑bounded using verification techniques such as \alpha,\beta-CROWN (Xu et al., 2020).

Proof. If G \cap \mathcal{C} \neq \emptyset, there exists \theta^* with g(\theta^*) = 0 and h(\theta^*) \ge 0, implying L(\theta^*, \mu) \le 0 for any \mu, contradicting the positivity of the infimum. ∎

This certificate transforms a potential failure mode into a diagnostic tool: before deploying SRI, one can attempt to find dual certificates proving that the chosen alignment and safety constraints are architecturally incompatible, enabling early intervention.

