Chapter 4: Discrete‑Time Dynamics with Endogenous Dominance Gap

4.1 Stochastic Update Model

In practice, self‑improvement proceeds via discrete parameter updates:

\theta_{k+1} = \theta_k + \eta_k \hat{\Psi}_{\text{SRI}, k} + \xi_k,

where \eta_k satisfies the Robbins–Monro conditions (\sum \eta_k = \infty, \sum \eta_k^2 < \infty), \hat{\Psi}_{\text{SRI}, k} is the stochastic estimate of the SRI vector field from Definition 3.1, and \xi_k is zero‑mean noise with \mathbb{E}[\|\xi_k\|^2 \mid \mathcal{F}_k] \le \sigma_\xi^2. A verification‑bounded rollback mechanism rejects any update that increases the loss beyond a tolerance:

\tilde{\theta}_{k+1} = \begin{cases}
\theta_{k+1} & \text{if } \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\theta_{k+1}) \le \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\tilde{\theta}_k) + \delta_k, \\
\tilde{\theta}_k & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}

with \mathbb{P}(\text{reject} \mid \mathcal{F}_k) \le \epsilon_k \to 0 as k \to \infty.

4.2 Endogenous Non‑uniform Dominance Gap

Using the same \lambda selection as in the continuous‑time case, the surrogate gradient push satisfies

\mu_k = \langle \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}, \Psi_{\text{comp}} \rangle_{g_F} \ge \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } \langle \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}, \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}} \rangle_{g_F} \le 0, \\
\delta \langle \nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}, \nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}} \rangle_{g_F} & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

The key property is that \mu_k is endogenous—it derives from the actual gradient geometry at each step, rather than being a fixed hyperparameter.

4.3 Step‑Size Bound for Discrete Surrogate Preservation

Theorem 4.1 (Discrete‑Time Surrogate Preservation). Under the update rule with the \lambda selection above,

\mathbb{E}[I_{\text{rare}}(\tilde{\theta}_{k+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] \ge I_{\text{rare}}(\tilde{\theta}_k) + \eta_k \mu_k - \frac{L_I(\mathcal{K}, H)}{2} \eta_k^2 (\|\Psi_{\text{comp}}\|_{g_F}^2 + \sigma_\xi^2).

If \mu_k > 0, the expected surrogate is non‑decreasing provided

\eta_k \le \frac{2 \mu_k}{L_I(\mathcal{K}, H) (\|\Psi_{\text{comp}}\|_{g_F}^2 + \sigma_\xi^2)}.

Proof. Taylor expansion around \theta_k using the local Lipschitz property from Lemma 2.3. ∎

Corollary 4.2 (Asymptotic Step‑Size Stability). In the positive‑alignment case, \mu_k \approx \delta \|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F} \|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F} and \|\Psi_{\text{comp}}\|_{g_F}^2 \le 4 \|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}^2. The step‑size condition reduces to

\eta_k \le \frac{\delta \|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F}}{2 L_I \|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F}},

which depends only on the ratio of the two gradient norms, not on their absolute scale. As \|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}\|_{g_F} \to 0 near convergence, the bound remains stable provided \|\nabla^F I_{\text{rare}}\|_{g_F} does not vanish faster—a condition we call non‑degenerate retention, which holds when the rare event signal remains informative even after the main task is mastered.

4.4 Complete Discrete‑Time SRI Theorem

Theorem 4.3 (Discrete‑Time SRI). Under Assumptions 3.1–3.4, with step sizes satisfying the bound of Theorem 4.1, verification rollback with \epsilon_k \to 0, and the joint projection applied at each step, the following hold almost surely:

1. \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\tilde{\theta}_k) converges to a finite limit.
2. \liminf_{k \to \infty} \|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\tilde{\theta}_k)\|_{g_F} = 0.
3. \mathbb{E}[I_{\text{rare}}(\tilde{\theta}_{k+1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_k] \ge I_{\text{rare}}(\tilde{\theta}_k) for all k, and by Lemma 2.2 the true MI satisfies the corresponding high‑probability bound.
4. \text{dist}(\tilde{\theta}_k, G) \to 0 and \tilde{\theta}_k \in \mathcal{C} with probability at least 1 - p_{\text{fail}}.

Proof. Combine the supermartingale argument for \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}} (Doob's theorem), the surrogate preservation bound (Theorem 4.1), and the probabilistic CBF guarantee (Assumption 2.2). Convergence to a stationary point follows from the Riemannian Kushner–Clark lemma applied to the projected stochastic approximation, as established by Zhang et al. (2024). ∎

4.5 Convergence Rate under Riemannian PL Condition

Assumption 4.1 (Riemannian Polyak–Łojasiewicz). There exists \mu_{\text{PL}} > 0 such that

\|\nabla^F \mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\theta)\|_{g_F}^2 \ge 2\mu_{\text{PL}} (\mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\theta) - \mathcal{L}^*) \quad \forall \theta \in \mathcal{C} \cap G.

Theorem 4.4 (Linear Convergence). Under Assumption 4.1, with exact natural gradients, the SRI composite update satisfies

\mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\theta_{k+1}) - \mathcal{L}^* \le (1 - \eta_k \tilde{\eta} \mu_{\text{PL}})(\mathcal{L}_{\text{self}}(\theta_k) - \mathcal{L}^*).

With stochastic gradients and constant step size, the rate is O(1/k).

